# Git commits and pushes

## Permission (read this first)

**Running `git commit` is allowed and expected** whenever this rule applies. ForgeTrail **ship defaults** assume agents **do not** wait for the user to say “commit,” and **do not** leave completed request work unstaged by default.

What stays restricted: **`git push`** (and merge/rebase/publish) still requires an **explicit** user request. If the user asks to **leave the tree dirty**, skip commits for that work.

## End of each request (default)

When you **finish responding to a user request** and your work **changed tracked files** in this repo for that request, **before or as part of your wrap-up summary** you must **`git add`** the paths that belong to that request (only those paths) and **`git commit`** with a **good message** grounded in **`git diff --staged`** (see `.cursor/rules/commit-messages.mdc`).

- If there is **nothing to commit** for that request, say so briefly and skip.
- If other **unrelated** local changes exist, **stage only** what this request touched.

## Push and merge

- **Do not** run `git push`, merge/rebase, or publish flows unless the user **explicitly** asks.

## When the user opts out

If the user says **not** to commit, to **leave changes uncommitted**, or is mid-experiment, **do not** commit—summarize only.
